{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}{{ 'control_plane.purchases'|trans }}{% endblock %}

{#
	**Who has asked to buy what** (XIV-102).

	A customer presses a button in their store, the store installs nothing, and the
	request appears here for a person to act on. That is [XIV-64]'s separation —
	asking and happening are not the same event — applied to a purchase, and it is
	deliberately the same shape rather than a new one, so that a real payment
	gateway later slots in where the operator currently stands.

	**Every row is a *collection*, not a request.** The request itself lives in the
	customer's own database, because §4.4 grants a customer-facing instance no write
	privilege anywhere in the control-plane database; `tenant:purchase:collect`
	copies them across. So each row is as old as the run that found it, and this
	page says so beside every one rather than looking live. §8.11 settled that rule
	for the usage figures next door, and the reason is the same: a stale figure
	presented as current is worse than no figure.

	**There is no button on this page, on purpose.** An operator fulfils a request
	by installing the module — `tenant:module:install` — and the next collection
	observes that and marks the row done. A "mark as fulfilled" control would be a
	second copy of a fact the customer's own database already holds, free to
	disagree with it, on the one screen somebody opens to find out whether they
	still owe anybody anything.

	**And there is no name here.** The tenant-side row records who pressed the
	button; that person's name never leaves their database. §8.11 drew the line at
	*how much* rather than *what*, and a customer's own people are on the far side
	of it. An operator knows which company wants which module and reaches them the
	way they already reach them — which is worth saying out loud rather than
	leaving somebody to notice a missing column.
#}
{% block body %}
	<main class="container page-shell py-4">
		{{ include('@XiviControlPlane/_nav.html.twig', {current: 'control_plane_purchases'}) }}

		{{ include('_flashes.html.twig') }}

		{#
			The line to read before anything else, and only when there is something
			to read: somebody is waiting. Warning rather than danger — nothing is
			broken, a person simply has to do something — and absent entirely at zero,
			because a banner that always says "0 outstanding" is furniture and
			furniture is what the eye learns to skip.
		#}
		{% if outstanding is not empty %}
			<div class="alert alert-warning d-flex align-items-start gap-2" role="alert">
				<i class="bi bi-hourglass-split" aria-hidden="true"></i>
				<div>
					<strong>{{ 'control_plane.purchases_outstanding'|trans({'%count%': outstanding|length}) }}</strong>
					<div class="small">{{ 'control_plane.purchases_outstanding_hint'|trans }}</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		{% endif %}

		{#
			The same sentence the pricing screen carries, for the same reason: an
			installation that has never set PRICE_CURRENCY prices in nothing, so every
			figure here is a bare number. §8.6 refuses to guess a currency for a
			customer because a guessed one is wrong quietly, and the honest
			consequence is naming the variable — to an operator, who can go and set
			it. The customer's own screen deliberately does not, because they cannot.
		#}
		{% if currency is null and requests is not empty %}
			<div class="alert alert-info d-flex align-items-start gap-2" role="alert">
				<i class="bi bi-info-circle" aria-hidden="true"></i>
				<div>
					{{ 'control_plane.price_currency_unset'|trans }}
					<code>PRICE_CURRENCY</code>
				</div>
			</div>
		{% endif %}

		{% if requests is empty %}
			<div class="card">
				<div class="card-body">
					<h2 class="h6">{{ 'control_plane.no_purchases'|trans }}</h2>
					<p class="text-body-secondary mb-0">{{ 'control_plane.no_purchases_hint'|trans }}</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		{% else %}
			<div class="table-responsive">
				<table class="table align-middle">
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th scope="col">{{ 'common.name'|trans }}</th>
							<th scope="col">{{ 'common.module'|trans }}</th>
							<th scope="col">{{ 'control_plane.module_price'|trans }}</th>
							<th scope="col">{{ 'control_plane.purchase_asked'|trans }}</th>
							<th scope="col">{{ 'control_plane.purchase_state'|trans }}</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						{% for request in requests %}
							{#
								A fulfilled row is dimmed rather than removed. "Did we ever do
								this one" is a question somebody asks, and a list that answered
								it by having no row would answer it wrongly. It sorts to the
								bottom, which the repository does.
							#}
							<tr class="{{ request.installed ? 'opacity-75' : '' }}">
								<td>
									<div>{{ request.tenantName }}</div>
									<div class="small text-body-secondary font-monospace">{{ request.tenantSlug }}</div>
								</td>
								<td>
									<div>{{ request.moduleName }}</div>
									<div class="small text-body-secondary font-monospace">{{ request.moduleKey }}</div>
								</td>
								<td>
									{#
										**The price as it was when they asked**, not as it is now.
										[XIV-101] left that as an instruction rather than a
										suggestion (§6.5): a sale is never recomputed from the
										module row afterwards, exactly as an invoice keeps its own
										totals. Raising a module's price this morning changed what
										the next customer will be quoted and changed nothing here.

										Drawn as it is stored, with the code beside it and no
										locale formatting, for the pricing screen's reason: the
										figure a reader compares against a customer's own screen
										should be the same string on both.
									#}
									<span class="font-monospace">{{ request.amount }}</span>
									{% if request.currency %}<span class="text-body-secondary">{{ request.currency }}</span>{% endif %}
								</td>
								<td>
									<div>{{ request.requestedAt|date('Y-m-d H:i') }}</div>
									{#
										When they *first* asked, shown only when it is a different
										moment — which means they have asked again. That is the
										number that says how badly this has been left, and it is
										worth a line of its own rather than being folded into the
										one above.
									#}
									{% if request.firstRequestedAt != request.requestedAt %}
										<div class="small text-body-secondary">
											{{- 'control_plane.purchase_first_asked'|trans({'%time%': request.firstRequestedAt|date('Y-m-d H:i')}) -}}
										</div>
									{% endif %}
								</td>
								<td>
									{# Colour and the word, never colour alone — the tenant list's rule. #}
									{% if request.installed %}
										<span class="badge text-bg-success">{{ 'control_plane.purchase_installed'|trans }}</span>
									{% else %}
										<span class="badge text-bg-warning">{{ 'control_plane.purchase_waiting'|trans }}</span>
									{% endif %}
									{#
										The collection time, on every row, because every value to
										the left of it is only true as of this moment — the
										customer may have been served by hand ten minutes ago.
									#}
									<div class="small text-body-secondary">
										{{- 'control_plane.purchase_collected'|trans({'%time%': request.collectedAt|date('Y-m-d H:i')}) -}}
									</div>
								</td>
							</tr>
						{% endfor %}
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>

			{#
				How a request is answered, written on the page rather than assumed.
				There is no control here that installs anything, and somebody arriving
				at this screen for the first time is entitled to be told what they are
				supposed to do instead.
			#}
			<p class="text-body-secondary small">
				{{ 'control_plane.purchases_how'|trans }}
				<code>bin/console tenant:module:install &lt;tenant&gt; &lt;module&gt;</code>
			</p>
		{% endif %}
	</main>
{% endblock %}
